# 12. INFERENCE ENGINE

Implement a reusable inference service.

The service must:

1. Verify that a trained checkpoint exists.
2. Load the model once when the backend starts, when feasible.
3. Set the model to evaluation mode.
4. Validate the incoming audio.
5. Apply the exact preprocessing configuration stored with the checkpoint.
6. Extract the model input features.
7. Run inference using `torch.inference_mode()` or an equivalent safe inference context.
8. Convert logits to a probability distribution.
9. Return the top prediction and the probabilities for all eight classes.
10. Record useful metadata about the input and inference.
11. Return an informative error if the model cannot be loaded.

The top prediction must be the class with the highest returned probability.

All probabilities must be finite, lie between zero and one, and sum to approximately one.

Do not randomly select an emotion or return hardcoded probabilities.

## Prediction interpretation

The response should include:

- Predicted emotion.
- Probability for each emotion class.
- Model identifier and version.
- Original audio duration.
- Processed audio duration.
- Sample rate.
- Whether padding or truncation was applied.
- Inference duration.
- A note that the result is a model prediction rather than a definitive psychological assessment.

Use the term "model confidence" carefully. Softmax outputs are not automatically calibrated probabilities of real-world correctness.

Include a configurable warning for uncertain or out-of-distribution inputs. Do not present a threshold as scientifically validated unless it has been evaluated.

# 13. FLASK REST API

Implement versioned, documented endpoints under `/api/v1`.

## Health and readiness

`GET /api/v1/health`

Return application status, version, and whether the backend is operational.

`GET /api/v1/ready`

Return whether the model is actually loaded and ready for inference, along with a concise reason if it is unavailable.

The application can be healthy even when the model has not yet been trained. Readiness for inference is a separate status.

## Audio analysis

`POST /api/v1/analyze`

Accept an audio file using multipart form data.

Validate the upload and return the prediction, probability distribution, audio metadata, and inference timing.

Do not return a fake success response when the model is unavailable.

Use appropriate HTTP status codes for invalid uploads, unsupported formats, unavailable models, and unexpected server errors.

## Model information

`GET /api/v1/model`

Return:

- Model availability.
- Model name and architecture.
- Model version.
- Supported labels.
- Preprocessing configuration.
- Last successful training metadata, if available.
- Dataset metadata, if available.
- A clear indication when any information is unknown.

## Evaluation information

`GET /api/v1/evaluation`

Return evaluation metrics and artifact references from the latest genuine evaluation report.

If no report exists, return an explicit unavailable state.

## Analysis history

`GET /api/v1/history`

Return paginated recent analyses, newest first.

`GET /api/v1/history/<analysis_id>`

Return the details of one stored analysis.

`DELETE /api/v1/history/<analysis_id>`

Delete an individual history record where supported.

Persist metadata and predictions in SQLite. Avoid storing original audio indefinitely by default.

## API design standards

- Use a consistent JSON response format.
- Validate all inputs.
- Keep stack traces and internal filesystem paths out of user-facing errors.
- Log unexpected exceptions without logging full audio contents or sensitive data.
- Configure CORS only for the intended local frontend origin.
- Add request size limits.
- Avoid unnecessary global state.
- Keep database access and inference logic separate from route handlers.
- Document every endpoint and its expected response.

